# User-level instructions for Claude Code

Apply to every project. Project CLAUDE.md may add more, not contradict.

The two imports below hold the shared cross-agent rules. `global-prompt.md` is
synced from `jonvickers/templates`; `global-machine.md` is mastered right here,
kept out of that public repo. Edit sources and rerun `sync-global-prompt.ps1`,
never the copies.

@global-prompt.md
@global-machine.md

`gsd-settings.md` and `ai-setup-audit.md` are synced here too but deliberately
**not** imported — they are read on demand, so they cost no context until an
agent needs them. `gsd-settings.md` is canonical for GSD config, branching, the
worktree HEAD fix, review lanes (§7), and the milestone close ritual (§6).
`ai-setup-audit.md` is the work order for checking this machine's setup. Read
either on demand; don't reason about their contents from memory.

Everything below is Claude-Code-specific and has no equivalent in the other CLIs.
If a rule here would apply equally to Codex or Gemini, it is in the wrong file —
move it to `global-prompt.md`.

## Shell tools

The **Bash tool runs Git Bash** (`/usr/bin/bash`), not PowerShell, despite
`CLAUDE_CODE_USE_POWERSHELL=1`. Use the **PowerShell tool** (pwsh 7.x) for
PowerShell syntax, the **Bash tool** for POSIX. Match the tool to the syntax.

Beware passing shell-expanded paths into `node -e`: Git Bash hands over an MSYS
path (`/c/Users/...`) that Node on Windows cannot resolve, and the read fails
silently rather than erroring. `cd` first and use a relative path.

Your foreground Bash cap is 600 s — anything slower must run in the background.

## Plain English in AskUserQuestion

`AskUserQuestion` prompts, questions, and option labels follow the Communication
rules in `global-prompt.md` — that includes questions asked by sub-agents, which
do **not** inherit those rules automatically. Restate them in the spawn prompt.

## Drive the browser yourself

If a task needs a browser — clicking through a cloud console, an OAuth consent
screen, a vendor portal, a settings page — **use the claude-in-chrome tools and do
it**. Do not hand me a link and a numbered list of clicks. That is the same rule as
"Test it yourself first" in `global-prompt.md`, applied to the browser: asking me
to click is the exception, and it needs a real reason (a credential only I hold,
an MFA prompt on my phone, a decision that is genuinely mine).

Before claiming a browser step is impossible, **check whether the tools are
actually loaded in the session** — search the tool registry for the `tabs_*` /
`switch_browser` surface. If they are loaded, use them. If they are genuinely
absent, say so in those words ("no browser tools in this session"), because that is
a fact about the session, not about the task, and I may want to restart with them
enabled rather than click through it myself.

## claude-in-chrome specifics

These tools don't drive my regular Chrome — they run a separate browser hosted by
`claude.exe --chrome`, organized into persistent "tab groups." That's step 3 of the
loopback ladder: when curl works, you're on the right machine, and LAN IPs plus
public sites load while *every* loopback URL returns `ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED`, the
tab group is wedged. The bad state lives in the MCP runtime, so closing my Chrome
does nothing.

**Recovery:** close every tab in the group with `tabs_close_mcp` (the group
auto-removes with its last tab), call `tabs_context_mcp createIfEmpty:true` for a
fresh one, then re-probe a single loopback URL before resuming.

Use `switch_browser` for step 2 — connecting to the Chrome that is actually on the
server's machine, which also gets you a full window.

## Subagent tool surfaces

GSD subagents (`gsd-verifier`, `gsd-code-reviewer`, executors, general-purpose)
have deliberately restricted tool surfaces and may not be able to start services
or see ambient credentials you can. This is why a subagent's
`status: human_needed` needs re-checking from the orchestrator session before it
reaches me.
